/*****************************************************************************
    kantParse1 -- LOOP CLOSURE, STEPS 2-5: A RULE THAT ACTUALLY PARSES, WITH
    ITS METHOD WRITTEN IN KANT AND SPELLED AS AN AND CHAIN.
    2026-08-11, SEQ 54.

    THE RULE. ScafKB is `"["- "]"-` -- two noLabel literal terms, the smallest
    shape that has a chain to short circuit and input to consume. Its parse
    method is kant CodE, found by the trampoline's convention (rule ScafKB ->
    action kpScafKB), and its body is one statement:

        return litK(1) AND litK(2);

    ⚠ THE ORACLE IS THE C++ ARM OF THE SAME SHAPE, and that is why this rule
    is a pair of literals rather than something more interesting. Scaf2 is
    `"{"- "}"-` and is bound to the EMITTED C++ parseScaf2. So the two arms
    parse the same shape, fail in the same place, and their Invariant R
    reports can be compared line for line. A fixture that only asserted the
    kant arm's verdict would be asserting against nothing.

    ⚠⚠ THIS IS THE CURSOR-ASSERTING FIXTURE the standing rule (SEQ 50 item 3)
    requires, and the assertion is on the FAILING row, not the passing one.
    Row 2 feeds `[` alone: the first term matches and CONSUMES, the second
    fails, the chain short circuits, and the frame must put the mark back. A
    verdict-only check passes whether or not the mark comes home -- which is
    exactly the eager-|| failure kantRuleS measured, where both spellings
    returned SUCCESS and only the cursor told them apart. The mark is
    C++-owned by Tony's 2026-08-11 ruling, so the observable is leaveRule's
    own R line, printed by the frame in the same words, and the assertion is
    that the KANT arm and the C++ arm say the same thing.

    WHAT EACH ROW IS FOR:
      1  ScafKB '[]'   both terms match          -> WIN, input consumed
      2  ScafKB '['    second term fails         -> FAIL, R OK mark rewound
      3  Scaf2  '{'    THE ORACLE, C++ arm       -> FAIL, R OK mark rewound
      4  ScafKB 'x'    FIRST term fails          -> FAIL, nothing consumed,
                                                    so the mark was never moved

    ⚠ ROWS 2 AND 4 ARE A PAIR AND NEITHER IS REDUNDANT. Row 4's first term
    never advances, so a rewind there is free and proves nothing; row 2 is the
    one with ground to give back. leaveRule's own comment makes the same
    distinction ("whether the rewind had ground to give back"), and a fixture
    carrying only row 4 would report R OK on a frame that rewound nothing.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();

registry(cOMMANDs);
define parseMethod immediateAction=parseRuleMethod noPrint; ;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define parseTerms immediateAction=parseTermCount noPrint; ;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define traceParse immediateAction=traceParse; ;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define litK immediateAction=litK; ;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define parseRK immediateAction=parseRK; ;

/*  THE KANT PARSE METHOD. One statement. The AND chain is SEQ 50's ruled
    spelling, and it is sound here for the reason the respell charter gives:
    short circuit stops at the first failed term, and the mark restore lives
    OUTSIDE the terms in the frame, so stopping early cannot strand it.  */
register(KantParse1);
define
    kpScafKB code={
        return litK(1) AND litK(2);
        };
    ;

register(Ladder);
define
    ScafKB isRule "["- "]"- parseTerms=2 parseMethod=parseViaKant;
    Scaf2  isRule "{"- "}"- parseTerms=2 parseMethod=parseScaf2;
    ;

search reset stack Grokking KantParse1 Ladder list;

traceParse('on');

print "=== ROW 1: ScafKB '[]' -- both terms match, kant arm ===":;
ScafKB('[]');

print "=== ROW 2: ScafKB '[' -- second term FAILS, mark must come home ===":;
ScafKB('[');

print "=== ROW 3: Scaf2 '{' -- THE ORACLE, same shape on the C++ arm ===":;
Scaf2('{');

print "=== ROW 4: ScafKB 'x' -- FIRST term fails, nothing was consumed ===":;
ScafKB('x');

print "KANTPARSE1 SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
